## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-tenant routing through ConfigMaps, path rewriting, and versioning with safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Namespace-scoped ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Request rewriting via tenant header
- Versioning strategy and default fallback
- Minimal YAML manifests for end-to-end validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo rewrite function
function rewrite(req, tenantId, version, mapping) {
  const base = mapping[tenantId] || mapping['default'];
  req.url = `/v${version || '1'}/api${req.path}`;
  req.headers['Accept'] = 'application/json';
  req.headers['Host'] = base.host;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test unknown tenants and version upgrades?
- How to handle partial mappings and rollback?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to securely manage per-tenant TLS credentials in a CNF gateway, including secret-driven config, hot-reload of TLS material, and per-tenant mTLS verification.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes Secrets mounted as TLS material for sidecars
- Hot-reload of TLS credentials without pod restart
- Mutual TLS with per-tenant CA certificates
- Rotation cadence, error handling, and tenant isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo example: load TLS certs from mounted files and reload on change
let tlsContext = tls.createSecureContext({
  ca: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/ca.pem'),
  cert: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/cert.pem'),
  key: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/key.pem')
});

// watch secret mount and refresh TLS context
fs.watch('/mnt/cnf-tls', () => {
  tlsContext = tls.createSecureContext({
    ca: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/ca.pem'),
    cert: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/cert.pem'),
    key: fs.readFileSync('/mnt/cnf-tls/key.pem')
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate certificate expiry and revocation in this flow?
- What are the trade-offs between in-cluster secret rotation vs external secret management (e.g., Vault) for CNF gateways?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a comprehensive cross-architecture supply chain security and runtime trust workflow that addresses edge deployment challenges, including offline scenarios and real-time policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Per-architecture image signing and SBOM provenance tracking
- Runtime attestation with architecture-aware claim verification
- Policy enforcement using OPA Gatekeeper with architecture-specific constraints
- Edge connectivity management and canary-driven rollback mechanisms

## Code Example
```javascript
function isAllowed(image, arch) {
  // Verify Cosign signature for specific architecture
  const signatureValid = verifyCosignSignature(image, arch);
  const sbomPresent = checkSBOMPresence(image, arch);
  const attestationValid = verifyInTotoAttestation(image, arch);

  return signatureValid && sbomPresent && attestationValid;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design per-tenant runtime feature controls using a Redis-backed flag store with TTL-based caching. It evaluates understanding of multi-tenancy, caching strategies, fallback logic, and operational observability in a CNF gateway context.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant feature flags in a Kubernetes sidecar architecture
- Redis key design using tenantID:path format
- TTL-based caching with 60-second expiration
- Fallback mechanism to global default flags
- Runtime observability via /flags/status endpoint

## Code Example

```javascript
// Feature flag evaluation in CNF sidecar
class FeatureFlagService {
  constructor(redisClient) {
    this.redis = redisClient;
    this.globalDefault = true;
  }

  async evaluateFlag(tenantId, path) {
    const key = `${tenantId}:${path}`;
    const cached = await this.redis.get(key);

    if (cached !== null) {
      return cached === 'true';
    }

    return this.globalDefault;
  }

  async getStatus(tenantId) {
    const flags = await this.redis.keys(`${tenantId}:*`);
    const activeFlags = await Promise.all(
      flags.map(async (key) => ({
        path: key.split(':')[1],
        enabled: (await this.redis.get(key)) === 'true',
        ttl: await this.redis.ttl(key)
      }))
    );

    return {
      tenantId,
      flags: activeFlags,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
    };
  }
}
```

## Redis Seed Configuration

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: redis-seed
  namespace: cnf-system
data:
  seed.sh: |
    #!/bin/bash
    redis-cli SET "tenant-a:/api/v1/features" "true" EX 60
    redis-cli SET "tenant-a:/api/v2/features" "false" EX 60
    redis-cli SET "tenant-b:/api/v1/features" "true" EX 60
```

